/*!
\page page_alamdmc_generic AlamDMC Generic Configuration

[TOC]

Generic configuration settings are applicable to all AlamDMC modules. 
As described in \ref alamdmc_format_basics, these settings are located in the leading part of the 
configuration file. It has its own internal structure, consisting of three main sections, as follows:
<ol>
	<li> \ref config_outputs </li>
	<li> \ref config_multiscale </li>
	<li> \ref config_search_procedure </li>
</ol>
	
\section config_outputs Configuration of outputs

The outputs of the program are controlled by three parameters:
 -# \confdef{output_prefix,path} is a prefix for the output files generated by the program.
 -# \confdef{state_request,logical} specifies whether the program incrementally writes out the material state variables on every deformation step. If \conffield{state_request} is `True`, the program will also write the initial state of the material. The texture data will be written to the file \conffield{output_prefix}.cur. Other outputs may vary depending on the model selected.
 -# \confdef{verbosity,integer} controls amount of print-outs sent to the standard output (the terminal). The value can vary from 0 (zero) for a minimalistic output to 4 (four) for very detailed print-outs about the calculations being performed by the program.

\par Example:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~{.cfg}
AKDQFF_UDSA          # Prefix for the output files
True                 # Flag: request incremental output of the material state
3                    # Verbosity level: 0 (the lowest) - 3 (the highest)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

\section config_multiscale  Configuration of the multi-scale model

Although the configuration options of the multi-scale model are pretty much fine grained, there are reliable defaults that the user can rely on. These defaults typically cover a set of aspects and allow the user not to define the corresponding collection of configuration options. If the defaults do not fit to user's needs, it is possible to adapt the model by selectively dropping the defaults. The multi-scale model is configured as follows:


<ol>
<li> \confdef{texture_file,path} specifies the path to the input texture file. The extension of the file will be interpreted to determine the format of the texture data. Currently the following extensions can be used: `smt`, `cur` and `cub` (case sensitive). Note that the CUR format allows many discrete ODFs inside a single file. If \conffield{texture_file} has the extension `cur`, the subsequent line must provide:
	<ol>
	<li> \confdef{texture_block,integer} provides the index of the ODF block inside the CUR file. The first block has index 1 (one).</li>
	</ol>
</li>
<li> \confdef{model_type,string} specifies the multi-scale crystal plasticity model to be used in the calculations: either `ALAMEL` or `FCTaylor` (case sensitive).
</li>
<li> \confdef{slip_system_family,string} defines the slip system families associated to a particular crystal structure. Supported values are: 
	<ul>
		<li>`fcc12`: the 12 slip systems of {111}&lt;110&gt; family.</li>
		<li>`bcc12`: the 12 slip systems of {110}&lt;111&gt; family.</li>
		<li>`bcc24`: the 12 slip systems of {110}&lt;111&gt; family, plus the 12 slip systems of {112}&lt;111&gt; family.</li> 
		<li>`bcc48`: the 12 slip systems of {110}&lt;111&gt; family, plus the 12 slip systems of {112}&lt;111&gt; family, plus the 24 slip systems of {123}&lt;111&gt; family.</li>
	</ul>
</li>
<li> \confdef{microstructure_default,logical} specifies if the default microstructural description should be used, namely equi-axed grained microstructure. If \conffield{microstructure_default} is `False`, the subsequent lines must provide the following options: 
	<ol>
	<li> \confdef{microstructure_file,path} specifies the file with microstructure definition.</li>
	<li> \confdef{microstructure_F,srtensor} defines a pre-deformation of the microstructure definition in the form of a deformation gradient tensor.</li> 
	</ol>
</li>
<li>\confdef{hardening_default,logical} specifies if the default hardening description should be used. As default, non-hardening slip systems are assumed having a critical resolved shear stress equal to 1.0, independent of deformation. If \conffield{hardening_default} is `False`, the subsequent line must provide:
	<ol>
	<li> \confdef{hardening_model,integer} specifies the micro-scale hardening law, in general as the dependency of the critical resolved shear stress \f$ \tau \f$ on one or more internal state variables. An example of internal state variable is the accumulated slip of a grain \f$ \gamma \f$. This is a multi-line option, which means that depending on the content of this field, zero or more subsequent lines are expected. 
	Supported values of \conffield{hardening_model} are:  
		<ul>
			<li> 0 (zero) \anchor hardening_model0 for non-hardening slip systems. This option is equivalent to the default hardening description.
			</li>
			
			<li> 1 (one) \anchor hardening_model1 for Voce-type of isotropic hardening. If this hardening law is chosen, the next line must contain five real values, being parameters of Voce hardening law: \f$ \tau_0 \f$, \f$ \tau^{III}_{sat} \f$, \f$ \tau^{IV}_{sat} \f$, \f$ \theta_0 \f$, \f$ \theta_T \f$.
			The classical Voce hardening law assumes that the hardening rate \f$ \theta=\frac{d\tau}{d\gamma} \f$ decreases linearly as a function of \f$ \tau \f$.
			Current implementation is more generic: besides such *one-stage* hardening behavior, also *two-stage* hardening behaviour is supported.
			The two subsequent hardening stages are denoted stages `III` and `IV`, respectively.
			In \f$ \tau-\theta\f$-diagram, the two-stage behavior shows 2 linear parts, connected to each other at a so-called transition point. 
			 The meaning of the Voce input parameters is as follows:
			- \f$ \tau_0 \f$ is the initial flow stress (i.e. of the stage `III`), 
			- \f$ \tau^{III}_{sat} \f$ is the saturation flow stress associated to stage `III`, 
			- \f$ \tau^{IV}_{sat} \f$ is the saturation flow stress associated to stage `IV`, 
			- \f$ \theta_0 \f$ is the initial hardening rate (i.e. of the stage `III`),
			- \f$ \theta_T \f$ is the hardening rate of the transition point between stages `III` and `IV`.

			The input parameters are subject to the constraints \f$ 0 < \tau_0 < \tau^{III}_{sat} \leq \tau^{IV}_{sat} \f$ and \f$ 0 < \theta_T < \theta_0 \f$.
			The one-stage Voce variant is retrieved by imposing \f$ \tau^{III}_{sat} = \tau^{IV}_{sat} \f$ and setting \f$ \theta_T \f$ to an arbitrary value within constraint boundaries.

			The two-stage Voce hardening law is given by:
			\f[ \begin{split}
				& \tau = \tau^{III}_{sat} - (\tau^{III}_{sat}-\tau_0) \exp{\left( \frac{-\theta_0}{\tau^{III}_{sat}-\tau_0}\gamma \right)} \quad \textrm{for} \quad \gamma\leq\gamma_T \\
				& \tau = \tau^{IV}_{sat} - (\tau^{IV}_{sat}-\tau_T) \exp{\left( \frac{-\theta_T}{\tau^{IV}_{sat}-\tau_T}(\gamma-\gamma_T) \right)} \quad \textrm{for} \quad \gamma\geq\gamma_T
			\end{split} \f]	
			\n
			Here, \f$ \gamma_T \f$ and \f$ \tau_T \f$ are two dependent Voce parameters that denote the accumulated slip and the flow stress at the transition point, respectively.
			</li>
			
			<li>2 (two) \anchor hardening_model2 for Swift hardening law (variant SwiftK). If SwiftK is chosen, the next line must contain three real values: \f$ K \f$, \f$ \gamma_0 \f$ and \f$ n \f$, which are the parameters of Swift hardening law given by the formula:
			\f[
				\tau = K \left(\gamma - \gamma_0 \right)^n
			\f]
			Only positive values of parameters are acceptable (constraints: \f$ 0 < K \f$, \f$ 0 < \gamma_0 \f$ and \f$ 0 < n \f$).
			</li>
			
			<li>3 (three) \anchor hardening_model3 for Swift hardening law (variant SwiftS). If SwiftS is chosen, the next line must contain three real values: \f$ \tau_0 \f$, \f$ \gamma_0 \f$ and \f$ n \f$, which are the parameters of Swift hardening law expressed by the formula:
			\f[
				\tau = \tau_0 \left(\frac{\gamma}{\gamma_0} - 1 \right)^n
			\f]
			Only positive values of parameters are acceptable (constraints: \f$ 0 < \tau_0 \f$, \f$ 0 < \gamma_0 \f$ and \f$ 0 < n \f$).
			</li>
			
			<li> 11 (eleven) \anchor hardening_model11 for Dislocation Substructural Hardening (DSH) model (variant described in _Peeters et al. ActaMat 49: 1607-19_).
			Slip systems that are supported in conjunction with DSH hardening, are:
			\conffield{slip_system_family} = `bcc24`.
			If the DSH model is selected, two subsequent lines are to be provided:
				<ul>
					<li> \confdef{hardening_parameter_file,path} refers to the file that contains parameters of the DSH model.
					Please see the example parameter file `DSHparaset.txt` from `examples` directory for the ordering, units and meaning of the parameters:
					\includelineno DSHparaset.txt
					</li>
					<li> \confdef{hardening_state_block,integer}   \confdef{hardening_state_file,path}  configures initialization of the material state variables 
					associated to disclocation substructure.
					If \conffield{hardening_state_block} has a negative value, the  state is initialized in the annealed condition
					(and the contents of the \conffield{hardening_state_file} file will be ignored).
					For simulation on predeformed material, \conffield{hardening_state_block} has a non-negative value:
					it refers to an index of material state block inside the \conffield{hardening_state_file} file.
					The index runs from 0, which denotes the first block in the file. 
					</li>
				</ul>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</li>
	</ol>
</li>
</ol>

\par Example:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~{.cfg}
AKDQFF.smt           # Path to the texture file
ALAMEL               # Model type: either ALAMEL or FCTaylor
bcc24                # Family of slip systems
True                 # Flag: use default microstructural description
True                 # Flag: use default hardening
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

\section config_search_procedure Configuration of the search procedure
This part of the configuration file controls how the program will search for the strain rate that corresponds to a requested stress state. A gradient-based Trust Region (TR) solver is used to find the strain rate \f$ A^\star \f$ that corresponds to the stress \f$ S^\star \f$:
\f[
A^\star = \mathrm{arg min} \left\| \frac{S(A)}{\| S(A) \|} - \frac{ S^\star }{ \| S^\star \| } \right\|
\f]
To minimize the objective function, the TR solver requires its Jacobian. The Jacobian is numerically approximated by the finite difference method. 

<ol>
<li>\confdef{solver_default,logical} specifies if default values for solver settings should be used. If \conffield{solver_default} is `False`, the subsequent lines must provide the following options:
	<ol>
	<li> \confdef{jacobian_fd_step,real} controls how the Jacobian is constructed.  The field \conffield{jacobian_fd_step} specifies the length of strain increment that is be used for estimating the Jacobian by finite difference method. 
	</li>
	<li> \confdef{search_linearize,logical} specifies whether the program should first attempt to solve the minimization problem that is considered linear. Note that if the \conffield{search_linearize} is set to `True`, the following applies:
	-# the linearization is attempted each time when strain rate corresponding to a given stress is sought,  
	-# the linearization is done per strain increment,
	-# if the solver fails to find a good quality solution of the linearized problem, it will fall back to the non-linear problem.
	The non-linear problem is much more computationally expensive, since it requires several recalculations of the Jacobian.
	Therefore, the user is advised to use \conffield{search_linearize} set to `True` whenever possible. The linearized problem can be solved much faster and in practice its solution rarely differs from the solution of its non-linear counterpart.
	</li>
	<li> \confdef{TR_default_epsilon,real} \confdef{TR_objective_norm,real} control convergence criteria used by the solver. The search for the minimum is considered successful if the square norm of the objective function does not exceed \conffield{TR_objective_norm}. 
	</li>
	<li> \confdef{solver_advanced,logical} specifies if advanced solver settings should be read. This unlocks a multi-line option, which currently remains undocumented.
	</li>
	</ol>
</li>
</ol>
\par Example:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~{.cfg}
False              # Flag: use default solver settings
5e-2               # Control over numerical estimation of Jacobian
True               # Flag: attempt to linearize the problem
1e-5 1e-3          # Default eps for TR-solver, eps for the norm of objective function: ||F||_2
False              # Flag: use advanced solver settings
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

